<blockquote>
<p>This article boasts detailed and intense explorations of Hell; it depicts the mental breakdown of those who visit Hell; and it indulges in the occasional badass demon-killing action scene (described via interview logs, as appropriate to an SCP article).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So everything here seems like a rather dry rendition of the Protestant imagining of Hell, and if I'm understanding you right then the harrowing details of the experience and demon-killing are essentially the selling points of all this. And I'm not totally convinced that's a viable model — disturbing visuals are fine as complements but they usually can't make a piece stand on its own.</p>
<p>Frankly I'm more interested in a fresh take on the Protestant Hell, or examining why the particular locations allow for travel to it, or why this infrastructure to visit Hell even exists. Lots of ways to play around with the set-pieces that I don't think are being taken advantage of here.</p>
<p>Additionally, are you planning to follow any central characters or discuss a particular conflict? I'm only getting the sense that there's this foray regarding the Chaos Insurgency (which hasn't&#8230; aged particularly well, if you don't feel familiar with site standards I recommend you read <a href="/top-rated-pages-this-month">some newer articles</a>) and then an incident where the Foundation realizes that doors work both ways.</p>
<p>If you're unfamiliar, by the way, Hell and demons have already been used somewhat on the site, particularly in the mini-canon of stories surrounding <a href="/scp-4661">SCP-4661</a> and with the GOI <a href="/arcadia-hub">Arcadia</a>. I'd recommend reading through some of those stories to see what's been done and where you can expand the idea.</p>

							</div>

								
					
